1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150045 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4af94f20-7dad-5307-99ba-1ce48715b2c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150071 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186150072 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150071 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186150073 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150071 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186150074 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150071 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186150075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150071 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186150076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150071 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150071 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150071 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186150075 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150071 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "92ba2275-3203-5d26-ae0c-577da6431af2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150072 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150072 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150072 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a1809cb8-fdc2-5574-8f0a-5a6c7ab0cb37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150073 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150073 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150073 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "abc4ca59-2348-58fc-ab82-f9db7daa69e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150074 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150074 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150074 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "bc9a2ec5-9d5a-53f9-9125-061ed3b6d2b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150075 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150075 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150075 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d935d618-f8fe-5fd5-8492-932d981427ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150076 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150076 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186150076 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "77d44d72-e2f8-5440-ac87-43247e745bd2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151973 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6683" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151973 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown below. Which of the points shown are the local maxima of the function?\n\n![](images/24/245ba0a543188d50e52c23c854f9f82290790ed715c241bf27e505b04d857a4e.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151973 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "986cc1f3-747e-53cb-845d-411a21c9bc23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151973 #edb/ref 1074 "Consider the point $U.$ We can take an open interval of $x$-values such that $U$ will be the highest point on the graph in this interval. So, $U$ is a local maximum.\n\nA similar interval exists for the point $W,$ too. So, $W$ is also a local maximum.\n\n![](images/ac/ac0a6e28e4564883e6743b2d600cb8611723e813e85c1acb5fcc327ee3dbc9b5.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152026 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7970" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152026 #edb/ref 1071 "For which of the following values of $x$ does the function $y = f(x),$ shown below, attain a local minimum?\n\nI. $x=0$\n\nII. $x=1$\n\nIII. $x=3$\n\n![](images/17/1718b48d39255b221037a439cc48d300a04c33ffb4e04c700fefb303a20ebff2.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152026 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fa76f558-adeb-5d8d-9273-70f2d8a76f97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152026 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the given points in turn.\n\n- The function has a local minimum at $x=0.$ For example, in the interval $(-0.5,0.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/e2/e25e6e31d6ea5d7f882d4eed16c82ec8280d1ed56f275974e8f07091c0e62c5d.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x) \\ge f(0) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (-0.5,0.5).$\n\n- The function does *not* have a local minimum at $x=1.$ For any open interval containing $x=1$, there will be points on the graph that lie below $f(1)$.\n\n- The function has a local minimum at $x=3.$ For example, in the interval $(2.5,3.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/d1/d133d7ee4c022b47740f62b8e73ba9ea2f70e788861c25d2824b5e7236027c60.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x) \\ge f(3) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (2.5,3.5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152027 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7971" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152027 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the function shown below. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=-2$\n\nII. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=2$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=0$\n\n![](images/9b/9b933b1b869b74f2a7340f9ba4034b970c49434d12592d3ff1e152057b6adef1.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152027 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2cfd4563-e033-50c6-8e39-55295e81b4da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152027 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function has a local minimum at $x=-2.$ For example, in the interval $(-2.5,-1.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=-2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/3a/3abb7470db51fa1bdb61e15aec1739de44bf5083b7341b90a65ac93fc7f8adbb.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x) \\ge f(-2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (-2.5,-1.5).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=2.$ For example, in the interval $(1.5,2.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/71/71c6e7af0a1de77543b57d2aee242fe84295ad46c748b2f65f7cba381c47b80c.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x) \\ge f(2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (1.5,2.5).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=0.$ For example, in the interval $(-0.5,0.5),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/83/83de300c3e00766d46989d12b4e1708ef9a73f4d02748009a526b1646d7f0b81.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x) \\le f(0) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (-0.5,0.5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152028 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7972" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152028 #edb/ref 1071 "The graph of a function $y = f(x)$ is shown below. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=2$\n\nII. $f(x)$ attains a **global** maximum at $x=0$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=-2$\n\n![](images/91/91d9d4997dd58c2c3cb07efc1529487bbd9a5a2f5c611054289cfc2ba24d764c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152028 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10bb268e-60a2-5e88-9c78-f26dc11db1a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152028 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=2.$ For example, in the interval $(1.5,2.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/00/00a21a670b2080ce766ddf142eb2f37303d7af7b667e1b5038eee93879c071f7.png)\n\n \nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x) \\ge f(2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (1.5,2.5).$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function does *not* have a global maximum at $x=0.$ The global maximum is the highest point on the graph, but our function has points where its value is greater than $f(0).$ For example, $f(0) \\lt f(3).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=-2.$ For example, in the interval $(-2.5,-1.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=-2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/fc/fc96fe5bb938b0ca8569b864588348a4d22280e3c4a5806cdd20af6eb4bcea34.png)\n\n \nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x) \\ge f(-2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (-2.5,-1.5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152178 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152178 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152588 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152178 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152178 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152178 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Function Visually" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152178 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152178 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "e049969f-d389-5d67-a96c-4e0430942925" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152216 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152591 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152216 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152216 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186548990 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152216 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186549033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152216 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186549062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152216 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Function Using a Condition" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152216 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186152026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152216 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "05fd062c-7474-5f4d-aa40-06913fb420df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152217 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152217 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152217 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186549004 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152217 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186549035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152217 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying True Statements Regarding a Function's Local Extrema" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152217 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186152027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152217 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f9296cdb-31d8-5855-b605-a47c33b21c85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152218 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152218 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152867 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152218 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152218 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186549060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152218 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186549066 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152218 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186549068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152218 #edb/ref 1039 "Global vs. Local Extrema" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152218 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186152028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152218 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4c98ce69-8410-51a4-8d9c-eec7b46fb83b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152350 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152350 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152350 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152350 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152350 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152350 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152350 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152350 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152350 #edb/ref 1045 "Local Extrema of Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152350 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186153084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152350 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050033 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152350 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152350 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "69f74c8a-9d6c-5d27-b2eb-12f32044cc0f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152589 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152589 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152589 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152589 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/20/20b6e19fcb7e1fe4f33697d517c28d9563c793634cfbcd5552d842efeef3ef61.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are the local maxima of the function?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152589 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28dd7636-555e-56f2-95d9-58a422ebb093" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152589 #edb/ref 1074 "Consider the point $B.$ We can take an open interval of $x$-values such that $B$ will be the highest point on the graph in this interval. Hence, $B$ is a local maximum.\n\nA similar interval exists for the point $D,$ too. Hence, $D$ is also a local maximum.\n\n![](images/26/260f30379f71c006b43ec6eb26200ced27a4b40aec938140d6a4cd06910ba02c.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152594 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186150071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152594 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37834" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152594 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152594 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c4/c46e88b9e044fccb91e0efa4ee981973a286e0335f58a70f7b1f9a856d9bfed4.png)\n\nFor which of the following values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x),$ shown above, attain a local minimum?\n\nI. $x=3$\n\nII. $x=2$\n\nIII. $x=-2$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152594 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "73bf3b03-b88e-5d7f-a6de-e6310c1e255b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152594 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the given points in turn.\n\n- The function has a local minimum at $x=3.$ For example, in the interval $(2.75,3.25),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/61/6115a87572fb93b9e3dcf69c75cd4a5efaae2dbbea90b28cb57cdd05b8db11b7.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(3)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (2.75,3.25).$\n\n- The function does *not* have a local minimum at $x=2.$ For any open interval containing $x=2$, there will be points on the graph that lie below $f(2).$\n\n- The function has a local minimum at $x=-2.$ For example, in the interval $(-2.5,-1.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=-2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/26/26acee415fa4a1038077d11f88b7c61c8b45f4aa857cc578af192e4085f7c005.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(2)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (-2.5,-1.5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153084 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153084 #edb/ref 1061 24347 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153084 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "85da6730-a1ac-55ee-945d-f7a140cd4455" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153084 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153085 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153085 #edb/ref 1061 24348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153085 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2870450e-ff69-57b9-a345-eb615a023b41" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153085 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153156 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153156 #edb/ref 1061 29746 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153156 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1237e192-4ede-579f-907b-fa8dee77c024" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153156 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152216 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153157 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153157 #edb/ref 1061 29747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153157 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c6ea2a5d-7d63-54f9-930e-d0df5623dea2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153157 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153158 #edb/ref 1061 29748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153158 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5e9dba89-e765-5561-bf39-1a13f654a602" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153158 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152218 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153159 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153159 #edb/ref 1061 29849 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153159 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a3a9ac9-b320-5641-ba01-883737be2301" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153159 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153160 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153160 #edb/ref 1061 30168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153160 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "994e317d-e873-5b6a-b054-ffe802b777c5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153160 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153362 #edb/ref 1064 "A **local extremum** (or **relative extremum**) of a function is a point that is either a maximum or minimum point of the function relative to an (open) interval in the function's domain. \n\nFor example, consider the function shown in the graph below. \n\n![](images/19/197834de138cb2e05a3af391bbeec1279ad8bfddfb277b881e5e3eeb49bcb73a.png)\n\nThe graph has a peak at the point $A$ where $x=0.$ We say that the function has a **local maximum** when $x=0.$ We also say that the point $A$ is a **local maximum point** of the function. \n\nThis means that we can take an open interval of $x$-values around $x=0$ such that $A$ will be the highest point on the graph in this interval. \n\n![](images/d5/d522b4b0a59ff328399493bc12a770af2d99a1587b2826accb9835d7463ef9ba.png)\n\nSimilarly, the graph has a valley at the point $B$ where $x=2.$ We say that the function has a **local minimum** when $x=2.$ We can also say that the point $B$ is a **local minimum point** of the function.\n\nThis means that we can take an open interval of $x$-values around $x=2$ such that $B$ will be the lowest point on the graph in this interval. \n\n![](images/e2/e2a92d4217e1eebcc724e48cca273d2e707731ded914207e59fff149b46b4dcc.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153362 #edb/ref 1065 4078 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153362 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "63ce69cb-9024-5fca-baae-f6f89fe530a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153362 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153406 #edb/ref 1064 "Can a point on a graph be both a local minimum *and* a local maximum?\n\nTo answer this question, let's consider the function $f(x)$ given by the graph below. \n\n![](images/0c/0cec91d9ec8e108ee4836e816aae63722fc4c09d997790eaf283cd3e2a9c4bc6.png)\n\nNow consider the point where $x=2.$ Our goal is to determine which type of extremum it is. To do this, we consider the values of $f(x)$ in the open interval $(1.5, 2.5),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/c6/c6cf1a6237c50d5c2b651937ca46b9ae52a08dafb831d76b27ddb068d37dd501.png)\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- The point where $x=2$ is a local maximum because $f(x) \\le f(2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (1.5,2.5)\\quad {\\color{\\green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- The point where $x=2$ is a local minimum because $f(x) \\ge f(2) \\:$ for all $\\: x \\in (1.5,2.5)\\quad {\\color{\\green}\\checkmark}$\n\nTherefore, $x=2$ is both a local maximum *and* a local minimum of $f(x)!$ \n\nThis might seem strange. But, notice that $x=2$ lies within an interval where the function is constant. This is a special case where a particular point is both a local minimum and a local maximum." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153406 #edb/ref 1065 4806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153406 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "5e6aefda-fcc2-5ef4-88df-41bc5e862aac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153406 #edb/ref 1067 "Special Cases of Extrema" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153407 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's go back to looking at the function $y=f(x)$ that we saw earlier.\n\n![](images/19/197834de138cb2e05a3af391bbeec1279ad8bfddfb277b881e5e3eeb49bcb73a.png)\n\nThe point $A$ where $x=0$ is a local maximum of $f(x)$ because we can draw an open interval around $x=0$ such that the point $A$ is the highest point on the graph. One such interval is $x\\in (-0.5,0.5),$ shown below:\n\n![](images/d5/d522b4b0a59ff328399493bc12a770af2d99a1587b2826accb9835d7463ef9ba.png)\n\nEverywhere in this interval, the value of the function is *less than or equal to* $f(0),$ the largest value of the function in this interval. We can write this mathematically as\n\n$\\qquad$ $f(x) \\leq f(0)$ for all $x\\in(-0.5, 0.5).$\n\nSimilarly, the point $B$ where $x=2$ is a local minimum of $f(x)$ because we can draw an open interval around $x=2$ such that the point $B$ is the lowest point on the graph. One such interval is $x\\in (1.5,2.5),$ shown below:\n\n![](images/e2/e2a92d4217e1eebcc724e48cca273d2e707731ded914207e59fff149b46b4dcc.png)\n\nEverywhere in this interval, the value of the function is *greater than or equal to* $f(2),$ the smallest value of the function in this interval. We can write this mathematically as\n\n$\\qquad$ $f(x) \\geq f(2)$ for all $x\\in(1.5, 2.5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153407 #edb/ref 1065 4836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153407 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "d3d44d79-c497-5b0e-a272-62a35d8b9338" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153407 #edb/ref 1067 "Writing a Condition for a Point To Be a Local Extremum" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548362 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548362 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548362 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "02383d20-0e98-5f57-be6b-6df75947ede2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548389 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548389 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548389 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0f56b3cc-115f-57ad-a4f5-2b4f7ee5bbfb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548390 #edb/ref 1080 "I and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548390 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548390 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0ff7a2f1-6a9d-58b0-9489-95fdb0edebdd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548401 #edb/ref 1080 "III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548401 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548401 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1b806a24-f26f-55a2-80e1-991117d5b9ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548432 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548432 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548432 #edb/ref 108